 and His Sons is a monumental marble sculpture attributed to the Hellenistic sculptor Polydoros, created circa 1 BCE, and housed in the Museo Pio-Clementino in Vatican City. This work captures a dramatic moment from Greek mythology, specifically the tale of Laocoön, a Trojan priest who, along with his sons, was attacked by sea serpents sent by the gods. Its creation reflects the artistic innovations of the Hellenistic period, characterized by emotional expression and dynamic movement, making it a pivotal piece in the transition from Classical to Hellenistic art. The sculpture's intricate details and emotional intensity have made it a subject of study and admiration for centuries, illustrating the cultural and artistic values of ancient Greece.

The narrative depicted in the sculpture draws on the myth of Laocoön, who warned the Trojans against bringing the wooden horse into their city, a warning ignored that ultimately led to their downfall. The dramatic moment of struggle portrayed in the sculpture signifies the tension between human fate and divine intervention—an enduring theme in classical literature. Furthermore, the work exemplifies the technical mastery of marble carving, showcasing the skill of Polydoros and the aesthetic standards of his time, as it combines realism with idealism, enhancing the viewer's emotional experience.

